**Goal:** The last phase before v1 is "done" per `docs/design.md`'s own exact bar (line 75): *"Dockerfile/compose, CI green, migrations run clean, tests pass, backend+dashboard buildable, tablet APK buildable. It does **not** mean provisioning paid cloud infra, deploying to a live customer-facing environment, or sending real push notifications — those need explicit sign-off."* CI green, tests passing, and both apps buildable are already true (Phases 1-9). This phase adds the one missing piece — Dockerfile(s) + compose, with migrations verified against REAL Postgres (every migration in this project has, until now, only ever run against SQLite in tests) — plus a README quickstart and an explicit punch list of what still needs a human decision (domain, VAPID keys, cloud provider, real-device GPS testing, and everything else design.md itself already named as deferred).

**Environment facts, verified empirically before this plan was written:**
- **Docker is available via `colima`, but colima was not running at session start** (`docker ps` failed with "Cannot connect to the Docker daemon"). Every session that touches Docker in this environment needs `colima start` first if `docker ps` fails — this is a one-time-per-cold-environment step, not something to script into the app itself.
- **All 9 existing migrations were run against a real, throwaway `postgres:16` container for the first time in this project's history and applied cleanly with zero errors** — including the trickiest one (`cb47af5f6a3a`, the visits idempotency-key migration, which needed `op.batch_alter_table` specifically because SQLite can't `ALTER` a constraint directly; Alembic's batch mode is forward-compatible and just runs a direct `ALTER TABLE` on Postgres, confirmed by inspecting the resulting real table via `psql \d visits` — the unique constraint, both FKs, and all indexes are present and correctly named). This was a real, previously-unverified risk (every prior "verified via SQLite PRAGMA" claim in this project's history was SQLite-only) — now closed.
- **A live app boot + real HTTP request against that same Postgres container also succeeded** (`POST /api/v1/auth/register` → 201, real JWT tokens returned) — proving the `asyncpg` driver path works end-to-end at the ORM/query level, not just that migrations apply.
- **Vite bakes `VITE_API_BASE_URL` into the built JS bundle at BUILD time**, not runtime (`import.meta.env.VITE_API_BASE_URL` is a static replacement, same as the dashboard's existing pattern) — the dashboard's Dockerfile MUST pass this as a Docker build `ARG` (set before the `npm run build` step), not a container runtime `ENV`. Setting it as a runtime-only env var on the built container would silently have no effect (the value baked into the JS at build time wins) — this is the one genuine Docker+Vite gotcha in this task, named here so it isn't rediscovered the hard way.
- **`backend/uv.lock` exists** (confirmed via `ls`) — the backend Dockerfile can use `uv sync --frozen` for reproducible installs.
- **`ghcr.io/astral-sh/uv:0.11.21` is a real, currently-published image tag** (verified via `docker manifest inspect`, matching this environment's installed `uv --version` — 0.11.21 — so the containerized build uses the same `uv` version already proven to work in this repo, not an unrelated pin).
- **The app has no auto-migration-on-boot logic** (`backend/app/main.py`'s `lifespan` only starts the in-process retention job runner) — migrations must be run as an explicit step before `uvicorn` starts. `backend/alembic/env.py` already reads `DATABASE_URL` from `app.config.get_settings()` — the same env var the app itself uses — so a container entrypoint just needs `DATABASE_URL` set correctly and can run `alembic upgrade head` directly with no extra wiring.
- **`backend/docker-compose.yml` already exists** (Postgres-only, for non-Docker local dev — `uv run uvicorn` against a containerized DB) and stays exactly as-is; this phase does NOT modify it. A NEW root-level `docker-compose.yml` is added for the full containerized stack (db + backend + dashboard) — the two serve different, both-still-valid workflows (quick local dev vs. "does the whole thing actually run in Docker").

## Global Constraints

- **Multi-stage Docker builds for both `backend/Dockerfile` and `dashboard/Dockerfile`** — a build stage with the full toolchain (uv/Python, or Node/npm), a slim runtime stage that only copies the built artifact. This is the standard, correct pattern (smaller final image, no build toolchain in the image that actually runs), not gold-plating.
- **Backend image runs migrations via an entrypoint script (`backend/docker-entrypoint.sh`), then execs uvicorn** — `alembic upgrade head && exec uvicorn app.main:create_app --factory --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000`. Using `exec` (not a plain shell call) so uvicorn becomes PID 1 and receives signals correctly for clean container shutdown — a real correctness detail, not stylistic.
- **Dashboard image is nginx serving the Vite build output, with a custom nginx config for SPA client-side routing** (`try_files $uri /index.html;`) — without this, a manager who directly navigates to (or refreshes on) `/zones`, `/tasks`, etc. gets a raw nginx 404, since those paths only exist client-side via `react-router`'s `BrowserRouter`. This is a real, easily-missed gap for any SPA served via a generic static file server, not a hypothetical.
- **The root `docker-compose.yml`'s `backend` service depends on `db` via `condition: service_healthy`** (a `pg_isready` healthcheck on the `db` service), not a bare `depends_on: [db]` — a bare dependency only waits for the container to START, not for Postgres to actually be ready to accept connections, which would make `alembic upgrade head` in the entrypoint racy. This is why the healthcheck exists, not decoration.
- **Real end-to-end verification is required, not just `docker build` succeeding**: `docker compose up` the full stack, confirm the backend container's logs show migrations applying and the server starting, hit a real endpoint through the exposed port (register a user, matching the pre-plan spike), and confirm the dashboard's exposed port serves real HTML with a working SPA route (not a 404) — mirroring this project's established "compiling isn't the same as working" standard from every prior frontend phase.
- **README quickstart and punch list belong in the root `README.md`**, appended as new sections — this file is already the entry point (`## Docs`, `## Session protocol` already exist there) and design.md doesn't call for a separate deployment doc.
- **Punch-list scope**: everything design.md itself already flags as needing real infra/customer sign-off before going live — domain + TLS (needed for the dashboard's push-notification service worker to function at all, per design.md's own violations/alerts note), real VAPID keypair (the app ships with empty `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY`/`VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` defaults today), a chosen cloud provider/hosting target, a non-default `JWT_SECRET` in any non-development environment (already enforced by `Settings`'s own validator — link to it, don't re-explain it), real-device GPS/background-location testing (the tablet app has only ever been build-verified, never run on physical hardware or an emulator — no emulator/device exists in this dev environment, a limitation named explicitly in the 8a plan too), photo upload storage backend (schema-ready `photo_path` column, no storage backend chosen — a Phase 4.1 note, still true), and the items design.md's own "Deferred to the merge-into-Atlas phase" section (line 65-67) already lists verbatim (real CRM/Sales linking, real Sales order values for `SALES_VALUE` goals, folding `reps` into Atlas's HR `employees`, Atlas's full RBAC, multi-tenant UX) — restate these here too since 10.3 asks for "what's left," and design.md's own deferred-scope list is part of that answer, not a separate concern.
- Backend conventions unchanged for any code touched incidentally; this phase is almost entirely new infra files, not application logic.

