- [ ] **Step 3: Write `backend/Dockerfile`**

```dockerfile
# backend/Dockerfile
FROM python:3.12-slim AS builder
COPY --from=ghcr.io/astral-sh/uv:0.11.21 /uv /uvx /bin/
WORKDIR /app
COPY pyproject.toml uv.lock ./
RUN uv sync --frozen --no-dev --no-install-project
COPY . .
RUN uv sync --frozen --no-dev

FROM python:3.12-slim
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=builder /app /app
ENV PATH="/app/.venv/bin:$PATH"
ENV PYTHONPATH=/app
EXPOSE 8000
ENTRYPOINT ["./docker-entrypoint.sh"]
```

The `PYTHONPATH=/app` line is cheap insurance, not strictly required — verified locally that bare `uvicorn app.main:create_app --factory` (invoked directly via `PATH`, not through `uv run`) already resolves the `app` import correctly when the working directory is the project root, since Python's interpreter puts the current directory on `sys.path` by default. This project's `pyproject.toml` has no `[build-system]` section (confirmed — it's a "virtual"/unpackaged `uv` project), so `app` is never `pip install`-ed into the venv as a real package; the import only works via this CWD mechanism. `PYTHONPATH=/app` makes that explicit and removes any doubt rather than relying on an implicit default, at zero cost.

Make the entrypoint executable before it's copied in (it's part of `COPY . .` in the builder stage, then carried into the final stage via `COPY --from=builder /app /app`):

```bash
chmod +x backend/docker-entrypoint.sh
```

